  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nNo major changes to the timing of convection associated with a cold \nfront moving in form the northwest later Wednesday. Upper \nlevel/surface low to track eastward from northern Ontario through \nJAmes Bay and into northern Quebec with an extensive cold front \ncutting through the Great Lakes. Today marks a transition into \nhigher dewpoints and increasing temperatures as return flow feeds \nthe moisture into the region as high pressure that has been over the \nregion shifts eastward. Instability should not be much of an issue \ngiven the higher dewpoints while a layer of drier/lower RH air \naround 625mb and 0-6km shear increasing to 40-50kts ahead of the \ncold front brings a wind gust potential in what will probably be \nline segments of storms forming and moving through the CWA beginning \nlate Wednesday afternoon. PWAT values also very high over 2 inches, \nbut storms should be progressive in terms of forward motion through \nthe region, so while heavy downpours can be expected, they should be \nof relatively short duration. Storms exit to the southeast by around \n06Z Wednesday night with clearing in the wake.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nModels hinting at a developing and trailing trough axis aloft \nThursday which could serve to hold up the forward progress of the \ncold front due to a weak surface low forming along the front as a \nresult. The result is POPs hanging on in the southern zones into \nThursday before finally exiting by roughly 18Z. High pressure will \nbe building into the Great Lakes from the west at this time, and \nwill return to dry conditions for the end of the week and heading \ninto the weekend. Temperatures knocked back 10-15 degrees with the \npassage of the cold front will be slightly below normal for Friday \nbefore gradual warming also returns for the weekend. With high \npressure now off the New England coast for Saturday and Sunday, \nsoutherly flow will reintroduce low end POPs for the area, but most \nlocations likely staying dry during this period. Another frontal \nsystem approaches late Sunday into Sunday night.",
